TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42997179
TI  - Cardiovascular-kidney-metabolic syndrome: novel molecular mechanism and glucagon-like peptide-1-targeting therapy.
AU  - Liu Fengzhao
AU  - Xu Zihan
AU  - Wang Wenru
AU  - Gao Junwei
AU  - Fu Changgeng
AU  - Liu Peng
JO  - Frontiers in cell and developmental biology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.3389/fcell.2026.1996288
AB  - Cardiovascular-kidney-metabolic (CKM) syndrome represents a systemic disease continuum arising from the progressive and reciprocal interactions among metabolic dysfunction, kidney injury, and cardiovascular disease. Obesity and type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) are major metabolic drivers of early CKM progression and contribute to cardiac and kidney injury through insulin resistance, glucotoxicity and lipotoxicity, chronic inflammation, oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, and ectopic lipid deposition, thereby establishing a self-reinforcing vicious cycle across the metabolic, cardiovascular, and renal systems. Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) and its receptor (GLP-1R) not only participate in the regulation of glucose homeostasis and energy metabolism but also represent an important regulatory nexus linking metabolic dysfunction to cardiorenal injury. GLP-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) improve metabolic homeostasis by enhancing glucose-dependent insulin secretion, suppressing glucagon release, reducing appetite and body weight, and decreasing visceral and ectopic fat accumulation. In parallel, they may exert renal and cardiovascular protective effects by improving endothelial function and microcirculation, suppressing inflammation and oxidative stress, maintaining mitochondrial homeostasis, and attenuating fibrosis. In recent years, large-scale clinical studies have further extended the benefits of GLP-1RAs beyond glycaemic control and weight reduction to improved kidney outcomes, reduced major cardiovascular events, and benefits in obesity-related heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. Meanwhile, natural products, characterised by multi-component, multi-target, and multi-pathway actions, may provide a complementary strategy for integrated CKM management by simultaneously improving glucose and lipid metabolic disturbances, insulin resistance, inflammation, and oxidative stress, while attenuating cardiac remodelling, kidney injury, and fibrosis. Emerging studies have shown that some natural products can concurrently improve metabolic, cardiac, and renal phenotypes in CKM-related diseases or composite disease models. This review focuses on the interactions among the metabolic, renal, and cardiovascular systems in CKM, summarises the biological basis and signalling mechanisms of the GLP-1/GLP-1R system, and highlights the mechanistic and clinical evidence supporting GLP-1RA-mediated protection against obesity- and T2DM-related metabolic dysfunction and cardiorenal injury. The potential value of natural products in comprehensive CKM intervention and future research directions are also discussed.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42997179/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42997027
TI  - The coverage gap in PREVENT: Why cardiovascular risk prediction needs a lab-free complement.
AU  - Harris Rebecca Arden
AU  - Bilker Warren B
AU  - Jacoby Douglas S
AU  - Levin Michael G
AU  - Wender Richard C
AU  - Hennessy Sean
JO  - American journal of preventive cardiology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.ajpc.2026.101782
AB  - The American Heart Association's PREVENT equations are now embedded in three major AHA/ACC guidelines governing hypertension, dyslipidemia, and cardiovascular-kidney-metabolic syndrome, including a specific risk threshold that gates initiation of GLP-1-based therapy and SGLT2 inhibitors. This rapid guideline uptake has outpaced a persistent limitation: PREVENT requires laboratory values, chiefly lipids and estimated glomerular filtration rate, that are frequently missing in the populations where risk prediction matters most, including patients seen in community health centers, emergency departments, and substance use treatment programs. Real-world validation studies across multiple health systems show that PREVENT underestimates cardiovascular risk most severely in these same subgroups, with the largest underestimation among Medicaid-insured, uninsured, and Black patients. Imputation, whether simple substitution or multiple imputation, does not resolve this problem because the probability that a laboratory value is missing is itself associated with the underlying risk the model is trying to estimate, a form of informative missingness that standard imputation methods are not designed to address. We argue that the field should invest in developing and validating a standalone cardiovascular risk prediction model that does not require laboratory measures. Such a model would function as a complement to PREVENT, not a replacement, providing risk estimates for patients without laboratory data and helping identify those who warrant expedited evaluation. As PREVENT becomes the gatekeeper for guideline-directed therapy across three clinical domains simultaneously, closing this coverage gap is no longer a methodological refinement but a clinical implementation imperative.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42997027/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42993255
TI  - Clinical pharmacology of antidiabetic drugs: an update.
AU  - Wichaiyo Surasak
JO  - European journal of clinical pharmacology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s00228-026-04219-z
AB  - This review describes the updated pharmacology of antidiabetic drugs and its translation into clinical applications. English language literature published from January 1, 1998 to July 25, 2026 was collected using PubMed, ScienceDirect, and Ovid. Research articles, reviews, and clinical practice guidelines were included for discussion. Currently, ultra-long-acting basal insulins are emerging, allowing less frequent injections. Glucagon-like peptide 1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs), tirzepatide, and sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitors (SGLT2is) have novel pharmacodynamic properties and clinical evidence of cardiorenal and metabolic benefits, supporting their important roles in individuals with type 2 diabetes (T2D) who have high cardiorenal risk. Because of their effects on weight reduction, GLP-1RAs (semaglutide, liraglutide, and orforglipron) and tirzepatide are approved for the treatment of obesity. Furthermore, SGLT2is (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin, and sotagliflozin) provide cardiorenal benefits, with supporting potential mechanisms, in individuals with heart failure and chronic kidney disease. Conventional oral drugs (e.g., metformin, thiazolidinediones, dipeptidyl peptidase-4 inhibitors, sulfonylureas, meglitinides, and α-glucosidase inhibitors) lack sufficient pharmacologic and clinical data on cardiorenal benefits, suggesting their roles as adjunctive therapies. Novel candidates, including triple incretin hormone agonists (acting on GLP-1, glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide [GIP], and glucagon receptors), GLP-1RA-GIP antagonism-based therapies, amylin analogues, and new dual SGLT1/2 inhibitors, show promising results for glycemic control and weight loss. Overall, a comprehensive understanding of the pharmacologic characteristics of antidiabetic drugs promotes appropriate drug use for the management of T2D and cardiometabolic diseases and helps avoid undesirable events. Ongoing investigations of novel approaches may contribute to improved patient care in the future.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42993255/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42991162
TI  - Sympathovagal Imbalance and Insulin Resistance: The Autonomic Nervous System as a Convergent Therapeutic Target for Breaking a Bidirectional Pathogenic Cycle.
AU  - Mohr Jan Alexander
AU  - Jiravsky Otakar
AU  - Rucki Lucjan
AU  - Jiravska Godula Bogna
AU  - Samiec Jan
AU  - Pekarova Anna
AU  - Jakubovicova Eva
AU  - Chovancik Jan
AU  - Novakova Marie
AU  - Pekar Matej
JO  - Diabetes, metabolic syndrome and obesity : targets and therapy
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.2147/DMSO.S604281
AB  - The autonomic nervous system (ANS) and insulin resistance (IR) are linked through a bidirectional pathogenic cycle established during the normoglycaemic, hyperinsulinaemic phase of metabolic disease, years before conventional glucose thresholds are crossed. This review identifies the ANS and IR as convergent therapeutic targets for breaking this vicious cycle, synthesising evidence from autonomic physiology, clinical diabetology, and neuroscience into an integrated mechanistic framework. Two mechanistically distinct arms sustain the cycle: a fast, potentially reversible functional arm driven by hyperinsulinaemia - mediated sympathoexcitation via hypothalamic insulin-signalling pathways, and a slow, progressively irreversible structural arm driven by hyperglycaemia-induced autonomic neuropathy. Experimental evidence demonstrates that inducible peripheral sympathetic blockade prevents overnutrition-induced insulin resistance, establishing sympathetic overactivity as a primary causal driver rather than a consequence. Cross-sectional data have identified cardiac autonomic dysfunction in normoglycaemic individuals with elevated 1-hour post-load glucose who meet no pre-diabetes definition, revealing a population undetectable by current screening criteria. Prospective data from a landmark randomised prevention trial confirmed that autonomic improvement independently predicts diabetes prevention beyond weight loss, with hazard ratios of 0.84-0.85 for incident diabetes. Cardiac autonomic neuropathy, detectable via heart rate variability analysis, is present in 9-39% of pre-diabetic populations and confers a pooled relative mortality risk of 2.14 across 15 studies. Therapeutic interventions targeting both autonomic rebalancing and insulin sensitisation - including structured exercise, bariatric surgery, vagal nerve stimulation, renal denervation, and metabolic pharmacotherapy - converge on identifiable nodes of this feedback loop, enabling stage-matched targeting. We propose a risk stratification framework integrating autonomic assessment with glycaemic staging, including a proposed "Stage 0" category for normoglycaemic individuals with elevated 1-hour post-load glucose, to guide early, targeted intervention when the cycle remains breakable.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42991162/
