TI  - Bleeding and Thromboembolic Outcomes Following GLP-1 Receptor Agonist Initiation in Patients With Type 2 Diabetes and Atrial Fibrillation Receiving Direct Oral Anticoagulants: A Target Trial Emulation.
AU  - Du Fanxing
AU  - Cicali Brian
AU  - Singh Ospina Naykky M
AU  - Ruzieh Mohammed
AU  - Schmidt Stephan
AU  - Jiao Tianze
JO  - Diabetes, obesity & metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/dom.71481
AB  - Patients with atrial fibrillation (AF) and Type 2 diabetes (T2D) require coordinated stroke prevention and metabolic risk management. Direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) are standard for stroke prevention, while glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) are increasingly used in T2D. In DOAC users, GLP-1RA initiation may alter body weight and gastrointestinal physiology, potentially increasing DOAC exposure and thereby elevating bleeding risk. We aim to investigate the impact of GLP-1RA initiation on risks of thromboembolic disease and bleeding in patients with T2D and AF receiving DOACs. We emulated a target trial comparing GLP-1RA versus dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitor initiation. We identified Medicare beneficiaries aged ≥ 65 years with T2D and AF receiving DOACs. Randomization was emulated using 1:1 propensity score matching. Primary outcomes include thromboembolic disease and bleeding. Per-protocol effects were estimated using inverse-probability-censoring-weighted Cox proportional hazards models. After matching, 930 patients were included in each group, with mean follow-up of 1 year. Mean age was 74.7 vs. 74.2 years, and 47.1% vs. 44.2% were women in the GLP-1RA vs. DPP-4 inhibitor groups. GLP-1RA initiation was associated with higher bleeding risk (HR 1.18, 95% CI: 1.03-1.36), lower all-cause mortality (HR 0.58, 95% CI: 0.36-0.95), and similar thromboembolic risk (HR 1.50, 95% CI: 0.22-10.43). Among DOAC-treated patients with T2D and AF, GLP-1RA initiation was associated with a less favourable bleeding profile but no evident increase in thromboembolic risk. These findings support careful clinical monitoring and further studies incorporating body weight and renal function to inform individualized treatment.
